{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Cross-tenant IDOR on all knowledge-base routes (read/write/delete of other organizations' KBs and files)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-tenant IDOR on all knowledge-base routes (read/write/delete of other organizations' KBs and files)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "All knowledge-base management routes (`/api/knowledge-bases/*`) perform authentication but no organization-scoping on object lookups. `knowledgeBaseService` queries Prisma by bare record ID, so an authenticated user from any organization can read, modify, and delete another organization's knowledge bases, folders, and uploaded files."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "All knowledge-base management routes (`/api/knowledge-bases/*`) perform authentication but no organization-scoping on object lookups. `knowledgeBaseService` queries Prisma by bare record ID, so an authenticated user from any organization can read, modify, and delete another organization's knowledge bases, folders, and uploaded files.\n\nCross-tenant breach of knowledge-base confidentiality and integrity: an authenticated member of organization A can enumerate knowledge-base IDs and read org B's knowledge bases (names, descriptions, file listings), rename/soft-delete them, and modify or delete org B's uploaded knowledge files. Knowledge bases aggregate a tenant's RAG document corpus, so this exposes another tenant's content metadata and allows destructive modification.\n\nAdd organization scoping at the service layer: `findById(id, organizationId)` must use `findFirst({ where: { id, organization_id } })` (the pattern already used by `credential-vault.repository.ts`), and `update`/`delete`/folder/file operations must verify the parent record's organization before mutating. Alternatively, enforce org checks in the routes and pass `request.user.orgId` into every service call.",
                "markdown": "All knowledge-base management routes (`/api/knowledge-bases/*`) perform authentication but no organization-scoping on object lookups. `knowledgeBaseService` queries Prisma by bare record ID, so an authenticated user from any organization can read, modify, and delete another organization's knowledge bases, folders, and uploaded files.\n\nCross-tenant breach of knowledge-base confidentiality and integrity: an authenticated member of organization A can enumerate knowledge-base IDs and read org B's knowledge bases (names, descriptions, file listings), rename/soft-delete them, and modify or delete org B's uploaded knowledge files. Knowledge bases aggregate a tenant's RAG document corpus, so this exposes another tenant's content metadata and allows destructive modification.\n\nAdd organization scoping at the service layer: `findById(id, organizationId)` must use `findFirst({ where: { id, organization_id } })` (the pattern already used by `credential-vault.repository.ts`), and `update`/`delete`/folder/file operations must verify the parent record's organization before mutating. Alternatively, enforce org checks in the routes and pass `request.user.orgId` into every service call."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Path traversal + git argument injection in plugin installation (installPlugins)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Path traversal + git argument injection in plugin installation (installPlugins)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The scope-plugin installation path (WorkspaceManager.installPlugins) joins an attacker-controlled plugin name into the filesystem without any traversal check, and passes an attacker-controlled git URL into git clone as a positional argument. An authenticated user with modify access on a business scope can make the backend git-clone a repository into an arbitrary directory outside the session workspace (arbitrary file write), and can inject git options such as --upload-pack to execute an arbitrary command."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The scope-plugin installation path (WorkspaceManager.installPlugins) joins an attacker-controlled plugin name into the filesystem without any traversal check, and passes an attacker-controlled git URL into git clone as a positional argument. An authenticated user with modify access on a business scope can make the backend git-clone a repository into an arbitrary directory outside the session workspace (arbitrary file write), and can inject git options such as --upload-pack to execute an arbitrary command.\n\nArbitrary file/directory write outside the session workspace as the backend process user (locally confirmed: repo cloned into /tmp/marker-plugin via a ../-laden plugin name), and arbitrary command execution via git argv injection (--upload-pack=touch /tmp/marker-uploadpack confirmed). Enables overwriting other tenants' session workspaces (cross-tenant integrity) and planting files into paths later executed or read by the backend/agent runtimes.\n\nValidate plugin.name and plugin.ref against a strict allowlist (e.g. ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$, no leading dash, no path separators) at the POST /plugins route and again in installPlugins before the join; reject any resolved target whose relative() from pluginsDir escapes it. Reject gitUrl/ref values beginning with a dash and pass them only after a `--` argument separator; allowlist the URL scheme to https/ssh. Apply the same validation to the DELETE plugin cleanup path, which rm -rf's join(sessionsDir, sess.name, '.claude', 'plugins', plugin.name).",
                "markdown": "The scope-plugin installation path (WorkspaceManager.installPlugins) joins an attacker-controlled plugin name into the filesystem without any traversal check, and passes an attacker-controlled git URL into git clone as a positional argument. An authenticated user with modify access on a business scope can make the backend git-clone a repository into an arbitrary directory outside the session workspace (arbitrary file write), and can inject git options such as --upload-pack to execute an arbitrary command.\n\nArbitrary file/directory write outside the session workspace as the backend process user (locally confirmed: repo cloned into /tmp/marker-plugin via a ../-laden plugin name), and arbitrary command execution via git argv injection (--upload-pack=touch /tmp/marker-uploadpack confirmed). Enables overwriting other tenants' session workspaces (cross-tenant integrity) and planting files into paths later executed or read by the backend/agent runtimes.\n\nValidate plugin.name and plugin.ref against a strict allowlist (e.g. ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$, no leading dash, no path separators) at the POST /plugins route and again in installPlugins before the join; reject any resolved target whose relative() from pluginsDir escapes it. Reject gitUrl/ref values beginning with a dash and pass them only after a `--` argument separator; allowlist the URL scheme to https/ssh. Apply the same validation to the DELETE plugin cleanup path, which rm -rf's join(sessionsDir, sess.name, '.claude', 'plugins', plugin.name)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Hardcoded bypassPermissions with bypassable regex denylist enables unapproved arbitrary shell execution on backend host via agent tool calls",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded bypassPermissions with bypassable regex denylist enables unapproved arbitrary shell execution on backend host via agent tool calls"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend's Claude agent runs with a hardcoded `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`. The only remaining guard is a small regex denylist (`dangerousCommandBlocker`) that is trivially bypassed, so any prompt the agent acts on — including instructions injected via retrieved documents, skills, or web content — results in arbitrary shell command execution on the backend host without any human approval."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend's Claude agent runs with a hardcoded `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`. The only remaining guard is a small regex denylist (`dangerousCommandBlocker`) that is trivially bypassed, so any prompt the agent acts on — including instructions injected via retrieved documents, skills, or web content — results in arbitrary shell command execution on the backend host without any human approval.\n\nArbitrary command execution on the backend server (the EC2 host running the Fastify backend, where the Claude CLI subprocess is spawned), outside the intended workspace directory and without any approval step. The executed process inherits the full backend environment, including Bedrock AWS credentials (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`) and an internal auth token (`AUTH_TOKEN`) explicitly injected into `options.env` — both readable via a single allowed command such as `env`. Host file exfiltration is equally unconstrained (`cat /etc/passwd` variants are only blocked by incidental regex matches, not by policy). Any authenticated user who can chat with an agent — or any attacker who can poison a document, skill, or fetched web page the agent reads — can trigger this.\n\nDo not hardcode `bypassPermissions` for host-side agent runs. Select a permission mode appropriate to the deployment (e.g. `default` or `acceptEdits`) so that Bash execution and out-of-workspace access require explicit human approval, and make the mode a server-side configuration decision rather than a constant in `buildOptions`. Replace the regex denylist with a positive allowlist enforced at the tool boundary: restrict the Bash tool to an approved command grammar (or run it inside a sandboxed container with no host credentials), canonicalize file paths (`path.resolve`) before the workspace-containment check instead of a raw `startsWith(cwd)` prefix comparison, and do not pass `AWS_*` credentials, `AUTH_TOKEN`, or the rest of `process.env` to the agent subprocess. Network egress from the agent should be restricted so exfiltration via `WebFetch` is not available to injected instructions.",
                "markdown": "The backend's Claude agent runs with a hardcoded `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`. The only remaining guard is a small regex denylist (`dangerousCommandBlocker`) that is trivially bypassed, so any prompt the agent acts on — including instructions injected via retrieved documents, skills, or web content — results in arbitrary shell command execution on the backend host without any human approval.\n\nArbitrary command execution on the backend server (the EC2 host running the Fastify backend, where the Claude CLI subprocess is spawned), outside the intended workspace directory and without any approval step. The executed process inherits the full backend environment, including Bedrock AWS credentials (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`) and an internal auth token (`AUTH_TOKEN`) explicitly injected into `options.env` — both readable via a single allowed command such as `env`. Host file exfiltration is equally unconstrained (`cat /etc/passwd` variants are only blocked by incidental regex matches, not by policy). Any authenticated user who can chat with an agent — or any attacker who can poison a document, skill, or fetched web page the agent reads — can trigger this.\n\nDo not hardcode `bypassPermissions` for host-side agent runs. Select a permission mode appropriate to the deployment (e.g. `default` or `acceptEdits`) so that Bash execution and out-of-workspace access require explicit human approval, and make the mode a server-side configuration decision rather than a constant in `buildOptions`. Replace the regex denylist with a positive allowlist enforced at the tool boundary: restrict the Bash tool to an approved command grammar (or run it inside a sandboxed container with no host credentials), canonicalize file paths (`path.resolve`) before the workspace-containment check instead of a raw `startsWith(cwd)` prefix comparison, and do not pass `AWS_*` credentials, `AUTH_TOKEN`, or the rest of `process.env` to the agent subprocess. Network egress from the agent should be restricted so exfiltration via `WebFetch` is not available to injected instructions."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
