          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "d4225bfbdfffc1284cffab92da93cdb405eaa36662a93c276dd33bf62c19833d"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.9",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "a6072715f915072500f3db8195d44a5d1a2c800d0e786974fdcc18eb04ed191c",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.9,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-20 13:45:00 UTC",
              "target": "super-agent/agentcore (AgentCore runtime container)",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-862",
              "impact": "Arbitrary unapproved shell command execution, arbitrary file write, and unrestricted network egress (WebFetch/WebSearch) inside the AgentCore runtime container for any instruction the model acts on — including instructions from prompt-injection sources: workspace files restored from S3 (skills, sub-agent definitions, `CLAUDE.md`), knowledge/RAG results fetched via the injected backend token, and fetched web pages. The container holds the workspace, the `AUTH_TOKEN` backend credential, and any container-resident credentials; the Stop-hook full S3 sync (`syncWorkspaceToS3`) then mirrors attacker-created or modified files back into the organization's persistent S3 workspace, persisting the compromise across sessions.",
              "technical_analysis": "`runAgent()` in `agentcore/src/agent-runner.ts` builds the SDK options for every AgentCore invocation:\n\n```ts title=agentcore/src/agent-runner.ts startLineNumber=283 endLineNumber=289\nconst baseOptions: Record<string, unknown> = {\n  systemPrompt: payload.system_prompt ?? undefined,\n  allowedTools: payload.allowed_tools ?? DEFAULT_TOOLS,\n  cwd: '/workspace',\n  permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions',\n  allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true,\n```\n\n`DEFAULT_TOOLS` (line 23) includes `Bash`, `Write`, `Edit`, `WebSearch`, `WebFetch`, `Task`, `Skill`. Hooks are registered **only** when S3 workspace sync is configured, and those hooks are `PostToolUse` (matcher `Write|Edit`, uploads the file the tool already wrote) and `Stop` (full workspace upload). A repository-wide search of `agentcore/src/` shows zero `PreToolUse` registrations — unlike the backend runtime (`claude-agent.service.ts`), which at least installs `dangerousCommandBlocker`. So in the AgentCore path there is no security inspection of any tool invocation: the model's tool call goes straight to execution. The prompt itself is attacker-influenceable through several untrusted channels: (a) `restoreWorkspaceFromS3` (`index.ts:46-53`) copies every object under the org/session S3 prefix — including skill `SKILL.md` files, sub-agent `.md` definitions, and `CLAUDE.md` — into `/workspace` before the run, all of which the SDK loads via `settingSources: ['project']`; (b) `index.ts:73-75` sets `process.env.AUTH_TOKEN` from the payload so the agent can query the backend RAG; (c) `WebFetch`/`WebSearch` pull arbitrary web content mid-run. Any instruction embedded in those sources is executed with no approval step, and attacker-written files persist via `syncWorkspaceToS3`.",
              "remediation_steps": "Port the PreToolUse guard chain from the backend runtime into `agent-runner.ts` as a baseline, then move beyond a denylist: default the container runtime to a permission mode requiring approval for Bash and out-of-workspace access (or restrict `allowedTools` per deployment), register a PreToolUse hook that enforces workspace containment via canonicalized path comparison, and restrict outbound network access from the container so injected instructions cannot exfiltrate data or fetch additional payloads via WebFetch/WebSearch. Ensure the injected `AUTH_TOKEN` is scoped to the minimum API surface (read-only RAG search) rather than a full member-role token.",
              "counterevidence": "Considered: the runtime does register hooks (PostToolUse S3-sync, Stop full sync), but neither intercepts tool execution; `payload.allowed_tools` could theoretically narrow the tool list, but no backend caller sets it (grep shows no producer), so the default full list applies; the container is a microVM, which limits host blast radius relative to the backend-host finding, but the workspace, the injected backend token, and any credentials placed in the container remain fully exposed; `WebFetch`/`WebSearch` have no egress restriction anywhere in the runtime. No control was found that gates or inspects a tool call before execution.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that `payload.allowed_tools` is populated with a restrictive list by the backend in real deployments would lower this; confirmation that microVM isolation plus IAM blocks all credential and S3 reachability would lower confidentiality/integrity impact.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Read `agentcore/src/agent-runner.ts` `runAgent()` and `agentcore/src/index.ts`: confirm `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` + `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true` with `DEFAULT_TOOLS` including `Bash`, `Write`, `Edit`, `WebSearch`, `WebFetch`, `Task`, `Skill`. 2. Enumerate all hook registrations: only `PostToolUse` (S3 sync of Write/Edit results) and `Stop` (full workspace sync) exist — there is no `PreToolUse` security hook in the entire `agentcore/src/` tree. 3. Note the untrusted-content entry points: `restoreWorkspaceFromS3` restores organization-controlled/S3-restored skill and agent files into `/workspace` before the run, and `index.ts` sets `AUTH_TOKEN` from the payload so the agent can call the backend RAG API. Under `bypassPermissions` the SDK executes any Bash tool call derived from these sources immediately — no human approval step exists in this code path. Local-only constraint prevented invoking a live Bedrock AgentCore runtime; the absence of the control is a complete static fact of the executed code path.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Cross-tenant IDOR in userGroups member removal and skill/MCP access-grant endpoints (no org scoping)\n\nThe user-group member and access-grant endpoints operate on bare group/skill/MCP IDs with no organization scoping. An authenticated user of any tenant can remove members from another tenant's user group and rewrite the full access-grant list (skill and MCP server group access) for another organization's skills and MCP servers."
